== strategist plugins
use: plugins
short: Inspect and evaluate Strategist plugin enforcement

== strategist plugins authorize
use: authorize
short: Compose authorization evidence for a governed target
long: |
  Evaluates runtime readiness, persisted Role→Weapon bindings, the two
  independent execution gates, and candidate write-target enforcement. The
  command does not invoke external providers or intercept writes performed
  outside the CLI. Use --json for stable machine-readable evidence.
flag: --approval-gate (string) default="pending" usage="Strategist Approval Gate state: pending, accepted, denied, or revision"
flag: --execution-gate (string) default="allowed" usage="local execution gate state: allowed or blocked"
flag: --json (bool) default="false" usage="print the versioned machine-readable authorization report"
flag: --mission (string) default="" usage="mission identifier to include in the report"
flag: --root (string) default="" usage="path to .strategist/ root (default: auto-discovered from CWD)"
flag: --target (string) default="" usage="candidate write path relative to the project root (required)"

== strategist plugins evaluate-write
use: evaluate-write
short: Report whether a write to --target is enforceably allowed under the active connector
long: |
  Classifies --target against the mission's active.yaml base_path
  (internal/plugins/policy.ClassifyWriteTarget), then checks that
  classification against the native runtime connector's own enforcement
  observation (internal/plugins/policy.EvaluateWrite).
  
  This is a scriptable, deterministic tool an agent embodying Sniper can
  invoke before writing, rather than relying only on the write_scope/
  approved_scope prose in roles/sniper.yaml — see
  .analysis/refined/20260830-skill-gaps-triage/analysis.md Cluster 1 (K03) and
  .analysis/refined/20260830-skill-gaps-followup/tasks.md item A4 for why a
  CLI-exposed check, not internal Go interception, is the right shape here:
  Sniper is a parent-agent-embodied native role, not a Go writer this binary
  can intercept.
  
  Exits non-zero when the write is not enforceably allowed, so callers can
  gate on it directly.
flag: --root (string) default="" usage="path to .strategist/ root (default: auto-discovered from CWD)"
flag: --target (string) default="" usage="candidate write path to evaluate, relative to the project root (required)"

== strategist plugins prepare-embedded
use: prepare-embedded
short: Ingest external-skills-source/ into the embedded plugin catalog
long: |
  Resolves every ORKA-shaped package under --source (each subdirectory
  paired with a strategist.yaml project-adapter sidecar declaring
  canonical_role/risk_score), verifies it, and merges the result into
  internal/embed/defaults/plugins/catalog.yaml plus generated
  internal/embed/defaults/skills/<id>/skill.yaml mirrors, recording exactly
  what was embedded in a committed lock file.
  
  This is a maintainer/CI operation on this repository's own embedded
  defaults (ADR-0032's pre-build ingestion pattern, generalized from Treasure
  Chest to role-slot skills — see
  .analysis/refined/20260913-embedded-skill-directory-catalog). It is not a
  per-workspace end-user command and does not touch .strategist/.
  
  Use --check in CI to fail non-zero on drift instead of writing.
flag: --check (bool) default="false" usage="fail non-zero on drift instead of writing (for CI)"
flag: --defaults-root (string) default="internal/embed/defaults" usage="internal/embed/defaults root to generate catalog.yaml and skills/<id>/skill.yaml into"
flag: --lock (string) default="external-skills-source.lock.yaml" usage="path to the committed embedded-skill lock file"
flag: --source (string) default="external-skills-source" usage="directory of ORKA-shaped skill packages to ingest"

